|~ `::any`, the kind beneath every kind, and what is declared under it:
   the refusal of a host's value JSON has no form for, and the words
   every value answers. ~|

:any
  |~~ Any is the kind beneath every kind, where the words every value
      answers live: `type`, `eq`, `doc`, `print`, `json`, `explain`,
      `elide`, `if` and their kin. A word that means the same for every
      value is written once here, and the walk from a name to its verb
      ends here, after the kinds of the subject.

      No value is of this kind alone, since `type` answers the kind
      above it, and every value answers the words listed here.
      ::snippet~(::any | manifest) lists the words of every value.
      ::link~(::qlang/verbs | doc)
  ~~|

:any/holdsType
  |~~ `type` lives on `::any`. ~~|
  ::law~(::any | manifest | has ::any/type)

:any/isTheKindOfNoValue
  |~~ No value's type is `::any`. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | type | eq ::any | not)

:any/JsonValueNotEncodableError
  |~~ A defect of a host, which no query meets: a value the host put in
      the scope that is of none of the kinds of qlang has no form in
      JSON. `json` writes such a value that runs as `<host-fn name>`,
      and any other is thrown to the host, past the fail track, out of
      reach of `!|`.
  ~~|

:any/desc
  |~~ Answers the subject under `::desc`, `5 | desc` answering
      `::desc(5)`, a value that ranks the greater first: a key of `sort`,
      `min` or `max` that answers it descends, `[1 3 2] | sort ~(desc)`
      answering `[3 2 1]`, and in a vector key it turns its own place
      alone, `sort ~([(/n | desc) /s])`. A value of every kind turns, a
      map among them, and `payload` reads it back. The key is code, a
      quote: `sort desc` turns the vector itself, which `sort` refuses
      as no key. A desc of a desc ranks as the value does, and a key
      turned in `max` finds the least. The call takes no value; a field
      turns by reading it first, `~(/n | desc)`.
      ::link~(::desc | doc) ::link~(::vec/sort | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/desc/answer
  |~~ The subject under `::desc`, which ranks the greater first. ~~|
  ::desc

:any/desc/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `desc` turns its subject and takes no value: a key turns a field
      by reading it first, `sort ~(/n | desc)`.
  ~~|

:any/desc/answersADesc
  |~~ `desc` answers the subject under `::desc`. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | desc | type | eq ::desc)

:any/desc/descendsASort
  |~~ A sort by `desc` descends. ~~|
  ::law~([1 3 2] | sort ~(desc) | eq [3 2 1])

:any/desc/turnsOnePlaceOfAVectorKey
  |~~ In a vector key `desc` turns its own place alone. ~~|
  ::law~([{:n 1 :s "b"} {:n 1 :s "a"} {:n 2 :s "c"}]
    | sort ~([(/n | desc) /s]) | map ~/s | eq ["c" "a" "b"])

:any/desc/readsBackByPayload
  |~~ A map turns, and `payload` reads it back. ~~|
  ::law~({:n 1} | desc | payload | eq {:n 1})

:any/desc/turnsTheWholeVectorWithoutAQuote
  |~~ `sort desc` turns the vector, which `sort` refuses as no key. ~~|
  ::law~([1 3 2] | sort desc
    !| [type /actualType]
    |  eq [::vec/sort/key/NotQuoteOrNullError ::desc])

:any/desc/ranksTurnedTwiceAsTheValue
  |~~ A desc of a desc ranks as the value does. ~~|
  ::law~([1 3 2] | sort ~(desc | desc) | eq [1 2 3])

:any/desc/turnsAMaximum
  |~~ A key turned in `max` finds the least. ~~|
  ::law~([{:n 1} {:n 2}] | max ~(/n | desc) | eq {:n 1})

:any/desc/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesAField
  |~~ A field given to `desc` is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~([{:n 1} {:n 2}] | sort ~(desc /n) !| type | eq ::any/desc/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:any/eq
  |~~ Answers whether the subject and the value the call gives are one
      value: of one kind, under the same tags, and equal part for part,
      the order of a map's keys and of a set's elements aside. So a
      keyword is not its string, `:a` and `"a"`; a vector is not the set
      of its elements, `[1 2]` and `#[1 2]`; a value under a tag is not
      its payload; and a number equals itself whatever its digits, `1`
      and `1.0`, while a sum of doubles may miss, `0.1 | add 0.2`. The
      value is computed against the subject, so two fields of one record
      are compared through a name, `:r / | /a | eq (r | /b)`, and in a
      condition the subject is the element, `filter ~(/status | eq
      "open")`. `ne` answers the opposite.
      ::link~(::any/ne | doc) ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/eq/answer
  |~~ `true` when the subject and the value are one value, `false`
      otherwise.
  ~~|
  ::boolean

:any/eq/value
  |~~ The value compared with the subject, any value, computed against
      the subject.
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:any/eq/value/NotGivenError
  |~~ `eq` compares the subject with the value its call gives,
      `5 | eq 5`; a call with no value is refused.
  ~~|

:any/eq/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `eq` compares the subject with one value and was given more:
      whether the subject is one of several is `:x / | [5 6] | contains
      x`.
  ~~|

:any/eq/comparesPartForPart
  |~~ Nested values compare part for part, a set without its order. ~~|
  ::law~({:a [1 #[2 3]]} | eq {:a [1 #[3 2]]})

:any/eq/keepsAKeywordApartFromItsString
  |~~ A keyword is not its string. ~~|
  ::law~(:a | eq "a" | not)

:any/eq/keepsAVectorApartFromItsSet
  |~~ A vector is not the set of its elements. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | eq #[1 2] | not)

:any/eq/keepsATagOverItsPayload
  |~~ A value under a tag is not its payload. ~~|
  ::law~(::Box[1] | eq [1] | not)

:any/eq/readsANumberByItsValue
  |~~ A number equals itself whatever its digits. ~~|
  ::law~(1 | eq 1.0)

:any/eq/comparesDoublesAsTheyAdd
  |~~ A sum of doubles may miss the number it is written as. ~~|
  ::law~(0.1 | add 0.2 | eq 0.3 | not)

:any/eq/comparesTwoFieldsThroughAName
  |~~ Two fields of one record compare through a name. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 1} | :r / | /a | eq (r | /b))

:any/eq/value/NotGivenError/refusesACallWithNoValue
  |~~ A call with no value is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | eq !| type | eq ::any/eq/value/NotGivenError)

:any/eq/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesTwoValues
  |~~ A call with two values is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | eq 5 6 !| type | eq ::any/eq/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:any/eq/keepsTheOrderOfKeysAside
  |~~ Two maps of the same entries are one value, whatever the order of their keys. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2} | eq {:b 2 :a 1})

:any/ne
  |~~ Answers whether the subject and the value the call gives differ, by
      the equality of `eq`, whose opposite it is: a keyword differs from
      its string, a vector from the set of its elements, a value under a
      tag from its payload.
      ::link~(::any/eq | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/ne/answer
  |~~ `true` when the subject and the value differ, `false` when they
      are one value.
  ~~|
  ::boolean

:any/ne/value
  |~~ The value compared with the subject, any value, computed against
      the subject.
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:any/ne/value/NotGivenError
  |~~ `ne` compares the subject with the value its call gives,
      `5 | ne 6`; a call with no value is refused.
  ~~|

:any/ne/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `ne` compares the subject with one value, `5 | ne 6`; a call with
      more is refused.
  ~~|

:any/ne/answersTheOppositeOfEq
  |~~ Two values differ exactly when they are not one value. ~~|
  ::law~([[1 2] [1 1] [:a "a"]] | map ~(:p / | /0 | ne (p | /1)) | eq [true false true])

:any/ne/value/NotGivenError/refusesACallWithNoValue
  |~~ A call with no value is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | ne !| type | eq ::any/ne/value/NotGivenError)

:any/ne/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesTwoValues
  |~~ A call with two values is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | ne 6 7 !| type | eq ::any/ne/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:any/type
  |~~ Answers the kind of the subject, the tag its value stands under,
      `5 | type` answering `::number`. The answer is a tag name, a value
      that reads the kind's page, `type | doc`, and compares,
      `filter ~(type | eq ::string)`. Every value answers: a literal its
      kind, `"hi"`, `:foo`, `[1 2]` and `#[1 2]` answering `::string`,
      `::keyword`, `::vec` and `::set`; null `::null`; a quote, a doc and
      a tag name `::quote`, `::doc` and `::tag`. A value under tags
      answers the outermost, `::Width(5)` answering `::Width`, while `is`
      walks the tags to the kind beneath. A failure passes over the
      step; `!|` catches it, and the error at rest answers the tag of the
      refusal it carries. The call takes no value: whether the subject is of a kind
      is `is`.
      ::link~(::any/is | doc) ::link~(::any/tag | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/type/answer
  |~~ The kind of the subject, its outermost tag. ~~|
  ::tag

:any/type/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `type` answers the kind of its subject and takes no value: whether
      the subject is of a kind is `is`, `5 | is ::number`.
  ~~|

:any/type/ofANumber
  |~~ A number literal is a number. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | type | eq ::number)

:any/type/readsThePageOfItsKind
  |~~ The answer reads the page of the kind. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | type | doc | eq (::number | doc))

:any/type/comparesAsATagName
  |~~ The answer compares with a tag name. ~~|
  ::law~(["a" 1 "b"] | filter ~(type | eq ::string) | eq ["a" "b"])

:any/type/ofAString
  |~~ A string literal is a string. ~~|
  ::law~("hi" | type | eq ::string)

:any/type/ofAKeyword
  |~~ A keyword literal is a keyword. ~~|
  ::law~(:foo | type | eq ::keyword)

:any/type/ofAVector
  |~~ A vector literal is a vector. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | type | eq ::vec)

:any/type/ofASet
  |~~ A set literal is a set. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2] | type | eq ::set)

:any/type/ofNull
  |~~ Null answers `::null`, a missing field read by `at` among them. ~~|
  ::law~({} | at :a | type | eq ::null)

:any/type/ofAQuote
  |~~ A quote answers `::quote`. ~~|
  ::law~(~(42) | type | eq ::quote)

:any/type/ofADoc
  |~~ A doc answers `::doc`. ~~|
  ::law~(|~~ A page. ~~| | type | eq ::doc)

:any/type/ofATagName
  |~~ A tag name answers `::tag`. ~~|
  ::law~(::vec | type | eq ::tag)

:any/type/answersTheOutermostTag
  |~~ A value under tags answers the outermost. ~~|
  ::law~(::Width(5) | type | eq ::Width)

:any/type/readsAnErrorOnceOpened
  |~~ An error passes over `type`, and its descriptor answers the tag of its refusal. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | add 1 | type !| type | eq ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError)

:any/type/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesAKind
  |~~ A kind given to `type` is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | type ::number !| type | eq ::any/type/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:any/json
  |~~ Answers the subject written as JSON text, for a reader that does
      not read qlang: `{:a 1 :b 2} | json` answers `{"a":1,"b":2}`.
      Every value answers, and the shapes of qlang alone give way at
      this boundary: a keyword writes as its bare name, a key and a
      value alike; a set as an array; a value under a tag as an object
      of its `$tag` and its `payload`, an error at rest among them; a
      quote, a doc and a tag name as
      the string of their print. So `parseJson` reads a keyword back as
      its string and a set as a vector: the text that reads back as the
      value is `print`. The call takes no value, an indent among them.
      ::link~(::any/print | doc) ::link~(::string/parseJson | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/json/answer
  |~~ The JSON text of the subject. ~~|
  ::string

:any/json/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `json` writes the whole subject on one line and takes no value, an
      indent among them: a field is written by reading it first,
      `/a | json`.
  ~~|

:any/json/writesAMapAsAnObject
  |~~ A map is written as an object. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2} | json | eq "{\"a\":1,\"b\":2}")

:any/json/writesKeywordsAsNames
  |~~ A keyword is written as its bare name. ~~|
  ::law~([:foo :bar] | json | eq "[\"foo\",\"bar\"]")

:any/json/writesAnErrorAtRestUnderItsTag
  |~~ An error at rest is written as a value under its tag. ~~|
  ::law~(::error{:k 1} | json | parseJson | keys | eq #[:"$tag" :payload])

:any/json/writesASetAsAnArray
  |~~ A set is written as an array. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2 3] | json | eq "[1,2,3]")

:any/json/writesATaggedValueAsAnObject
  |~~ A value under a tag is written as an object of its tag and its payload. ~~|
  ::law~(::Box{:a 1} | json | eq "{\"$tag\":\"Box\",\"payload\":{\"a\":1}}")

:any/json/writesAQuoteAsItsPrint
  |~~ A quote is written as the string of its print. ~~|
  ::law~(~(add 1) | json | eq "\"~(add 1)\"")

:any/json/readsBackAsStringsAndVectors
  |~~ `parseJson` reads a keyword back as its string and a set as a vector. ~~|
  ::law~({:k :foo :s #[1 2]} | json | parseJson | eq {:k "foo" :s [1 2]})

:any/json/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesAnIndent
  |~~ An indent given to `json` is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | json 2 !| type | eq ::any/json/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:any/print
  |~~ Answers the text that reads back as the subject, `42 | print`
      answering `"42"`, the text the command line shows for an answer
      other than a string, which it shows as it is. An atom prints as
      its literal, a string inside its quotes; a vector and a map by
      their brackets around the print of each part; a set, a quote, a
      doc and an error by brackets of their own; any other value under a
      tag as its tag before the print of its payload. A set prints in
      its one order, and the text reads back as the value by `parse`
      and `apply`. A kind may answer a `print` of its own declared under
      it, `:Box/print`, and the print of a value takes it for each part
      of that kind. The call takes no value: a print within a budget is
      `elide 80 | print`.
      ::link~(::any/json | doc) ::link~(::any/elide | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/print/answer
  |~~ The literal the subject prints as. ~~|
  ::string

:any/print/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `print` writes the whole subject and takes no value: a print within
      a budget of characters is `elide 80 | print`.
  ~~|

:any/print/printsANumber
  |~~ A number prints as its literal. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | print | eq "42")

:any/print/printsAStringInsideItsQuotes
  |~~ A string prints inside its quotes. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | print | eq "\"x\"")

:any/print/printsATaggedValue
  |~~ A tagged value prints with its tag. ~~|
  ::law~(::builtin{:a 1} | print | eq "::builtin{:a 1}")

:any/print/printsASetInItsOrder
  |~~ A set prints in its one order. ~~|
  ::law~("#[2 1]" | parse | apply / | print | eq "#[1 2]")

:any/print/readsBack
  |~~ The text reads back as the value. ~~|
  ::law~({:a [1 "x"]} | :v / | print | parse | :q / | apply q | eq v)

:any/print/takesThePrintOfAKind
  |~~ The print of a value takes the `print` of a kind for each part of it. ~~|
  ::law~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | :Box/print ~("box") | [::Box[1] 2] | print | eq "[box 2]")

:any/print/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesABudget
  |~~ A budget given to `print` is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | print 80 !| type | eq ::any/print/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:any/elide
  |~~ Answers the subject within a budget of characters of its print,
      what does not fit given way to markers under `::elision`, and a
      value whose print fits as it is. The largest part gives way first:
      a string or a map beneath the top gives way whole, the marker of a
      string keeping its first characters under `:head`; a vector keeps
      the first half of its elements, and half of those until it fits,
      ending with a marker of the rest; and a value under a tag keeps
      its tag, its parts giving way within. A part whose marker prints
      no shorter than it stays, `[]` or `{:n 1}`, so a budget never
      lengthens a print, and a value whose parts all print no longer
      than their markers is answered as it is. A marker's `:size` counts
      what it stands for, and its `:read`, applied to the value `elide`
      took, answers the part left out. The top gives way only as a
      vector, so a string or an atom alone is answered whole. The call
      gives the budget, a number; the command line applies `elide` to
      every answer it prints when `--budget=N` asks it to.
      ::link~(::elision | doc) ::link~(::any/print | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/elide/answer
  |~~ The subject within the budget, its parts that did not fit given
      way to markers.
  ~~|
  ::any

:any/elide/budget
  |~~ The budget, in characters of the print. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:any/elide/budget/NotNumberError
  |~~ The budget is a number of characters, `elide 80`. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:any/elide/budget/NotGivenError
  |~~ `elide` keeps the subject within the budget the call gives, the
      characters of its print, `elide 80`.
  ~~|

:any/elide/leavesWhatFits
  |~~ A value within the budget stays whole. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | elide 100 | eq [1 2])

:any/elide/elidesTheLargestPart
  |~~ The largest part gives way to an elision. ~~|
  ::law~({:s "a long line of text to leave out, which runs on long enough that its marker prints shorter than it" :n 1}
    | elide 40 | /s | type | eq ::elision)

:any/elide/keepsTheHeadOfAString
  |~~ The marker of a string keeps its first characters. ~~|
  ::law~({:s "a long line of text to leave out, which runs on long enough that its marker prints shorter than it" :n 1}
    | elide 40 | /s/head | eq "a long line of text to leave out, which ")

:any/elide/keepsAPartShorterThanItsMarker
  |~~ A part whose marker prints no shorter than it stays, so a budget
      never lengthens a print.
  ~~|
  ::law~([[] {:n 1} "short"] | elide 3 | eq [[] {:n 1} "short"])

:any/elide/halvesAVectorUntilItFits
  |~~ A vector keeps its first elements and ends with a marker of the rest. ~~|
  ::law~(["alpha" "bravo" "charlie" "delta" "echo" "foxtrot" "golf" "hotel" "india" "juliet"]
    | elide 80
    | eq ["alpha" "bravo" ::elision{:size 8 :read ~(drop 2)}])

:any/elide/keepsATag
  |~~ A value under a tag keeps its tag. ~~|
  ::law~(::Box{:s "a long line of text to leave out, which runs on long enough that its marker prints shorter than it"}
    | elide 10 | type | eq ::Box)

:any/elide/readsTheElidedPartBack
  |~~ An elision holds the query that reads its part back. ~~|
  ::law~({:s "a long line of text to leave out, which runs on long enough that its marker prints shorter than it" :n 1}
    | :v / | elide 40 | /s/read | :r / | v | apply r | eq (v | /s))

:any/elide/answersAStringWhole
  |~~ A string alone is answered whole. ~~|
  ::law~("a long line of text" | elide 4 | eq "a long line of text")

:any/elide/budget/NotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A budget that is no number is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | elide "x" !| type | eq ::any/elide/budget/NotNumberError)

:any/elide/budget/NotGivenError/refusesACallWithNoBudget
  |~~ A call with no budget is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | elide !| type | eq ::any/elide/budget/NotGivenError)

:any/if
  |~~ Answers the branch the condition chooses, run against the subject:
      `then` when the condition holds, `else` when it fails,
      `5 | if (gt 0) ~("positive") ~("negative")` answering `"positive"`.
      The condition is a boolean computed against the subject at the
      call, `if (/age | gte 18)`; a branch is code, a quote, so only the
      chosen branch runs, and the empty quote `~()` leaves the subject
      as it is. So the condition is no quote, `if ~(gt 0)` being
      refused, and null and a number are refused too, having no second
      reading; a field the record lacks answers its error, which
      `at :flag | eq true` asks around. Under `map` the subject is
      each element, `map ~(if (gt 0) ~(/) ~(0))`. The call takes the
      condition and both branches, and a chain of conditions is `cond`.
      ::link~(::any/cond | doc) ::link~(::any/coalesce | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/if/answer
  |~~ The answer of the branch the condition chose. ~~|
  ::any

:any/if/condition
  |~~ The condition, a boolean computed against the subject at the call. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:any/if/condition/NotBooleanError
  |~~ The condition is a boolean computed at the call, `if (gt 0)`: a
      quote, `~(gt 0)`, is code and no boolean, and null or a number
      has no second reading.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::boolean)

:any/if/then
  |~~ The branch run when the condition holds, a quote. ~~|
  ~(/1)

:any/if/then/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The then branch is code, a quote, `~("yes")`, run only when chosen. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:any/if/else
  |~~ The branch run when the condition fails, a quote. ~~|
  ~(/2)

:any/if/else/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The else branch is code, a quote, `~("no")`, run only when chosen. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:any/if/else/NotGivenError
  |~~ `if` takes both branches: a branch that leaves the subject as it
      is is the empty quote, `if (gt 0) ~(add 1) ~()`.
  ~~|

:any/if/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `if` takes one condition and two branches: a chain of conditions
      is `cond`, `cond ~(gte 90) ~("A") ~(gte 80) ~("B") ~("C")`.
  ~~|

:any/if/runsThenWhenItHolds
  |~~ The then branch runs when the condition holds. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | if (gt 0) ~("positive") ~("non-positive") | eq "positive")

:any/if/runsElseWhenItFails
  |~~ The else branch runs when it does not. ~~|
  ::law~(-1 | if (gt 0) ~("positive") ~("non-positive") | eq "non-positive")

:any/if/readsTheSubject
  |~~ The condition reads the subject. ~~|
  ::law~({:age 25} | if (/age | gte 18) ~("adult") ~("minor") | eq "adult")

:any/if/runsTheBranchOnTheSubject
  |~~ A branch runs against the subject. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | if (gt 0) ~(add 100) ~() | eq 105)

:any/if/runsOnlyTheChosenBranch
  |~~ The branch not chosen does not run. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | if true ~(1) ~(add "x") | eq 1)

:any/if/leavesTheSubjectByAnEmptyBranch
  |~~ The empty quote leaves the subject as it is. ~~|
  ::law~(-5 | if (gt 0) ~(add 100) ~() | eq -5)

:any/if/answersTheErrorOfAMissingField
  |~~ A condition that reads a field the record lacks answers its error. ~~|
  ::law~({} | if (/flag) ~(1) ~(2) !| type | eq ::proj/KeyNotInMapError)

:any/if/asksAMissingFieldByAt
  |~~ A field that may be missing is asked by `at`. ~~|
  ::law~({} | if (at :flag | eq true) ~(1) ~(2) | eq 2)

:any/if/choosesForEachElement
  |~~ Under `map` the subject is each element. ~~|
  ::law~([1 -2 3] | map ~(if (gt 0) ~(/) ~(0)) | eq [1 0 3])

:any/if/condition/NotBooleanError/refusesAQuote
  |~~ A condition given as a quote is code and no boolean. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | if ~(gt 0) ~(1) ~(2)
    !| [type /actualType]
    |  eq [::any/if/condition/NotBooleanError ::quote])

:any/if/condition/NotBooleanError/refusesNull
  |~~ A null condition is refused. ~~|
  ::law~({:flag null} | if (/flag) ~(1) ~(2)
    !| [type /actualType]
    |  eq [::any/if/condition/NotBooleanError ::null])

:any/if/condition/NotBooleanError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number condition is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | if 1 ~("yes") ~("no")
    !| [type /actualType]
    |  eq [::any/if/condition/NotBooleanError ::number])

:any/if/then/NotQuoteError/refusesAString
  |~~ A string given as the then branch is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | if true "yes" ~("no")
    !| [type /actualType]
    |  eq [::any/if/then/NotQuoteError ::string])

:any/if/else/NotQuoteError/refusesAString
  |~~ A string given as the else branch is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | if true ~("yes") "no"
    !| [type /actualType]
    |  eq [::any/if/else/NotQuoteError ::string])

:any/if/else/NotGivenError/refusesACallWithoutElse
  |~~ A call without the else branch is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | if (gt 0) ~(add 1) !| type | eq ::any/if/else/NotGivenError)

:any/if/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesAThirdBranch
  |~~ A third branch is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | if true ~(1) ~(2) ~(3) !| type | eq ::any/if/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:any/coalesce
  |~~ Answers the first answer of the alternatives the call gives, run in
      order against the subject, that is neither null nor an error:
      `{:nickname null :name "alice"} | coalesce ~(/nickname) ~(/name)`
      answers `"alice"`. Every other value ends the walk, false, `0` and
      `""` among them, and when every alternative answers null or an
      error the call answers null. So `coalesce ~(/key) ~(fallback)`
      reads "the key where the map holds it, else the fallback", since
      `/key` answers an error where the map lacks the key. An
      alternative that is no quote answers itself, and a call of such
      alternatives alone puts the subject first: `coalesce 30` reads
      "the subject, else 30". An error the steps before answered passes
      over the call, as over every step, so a field that may be missing
      is read inside an alternative, `coalesce ~(/key) 0`, not before
      it. The call takes one alternative at least.
      ::link~(::any/if | doc) ::link~(::any/cond | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/coalesce/answer
  |~~ The first answer that is neither null nor an error, null when every
      alternative answers one.
  ~~|
  ::any

:any/coalesce/alternatives
  |~~ The alternatives, in their order, each a quote run against the
      subject or a value that answers itself.
  ~~|
  ~(/)

:any/coalesce/alternatives/EmptyError
  |~~ `coalesce` answers the first of its alternatives that answers a
      value, so it takes one at least,
      `coalesce ~(/nickname) ~("anonymous")`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(count | gt 0)

:any/coalesce/passesOverNull
  |~~ A null answer passes to the next alternative. ~~|
  ::law~({:nickname null :name "alice"} | coalesce ~(/nickname) ~(/name) | eq "alice")

:any/coalesce/answersTheFirstValue
  |~~ The first answer that is a value wins. ~~|
  ::law~({:nickname "ace" :name "alice"} | coalesce ~(/nickname) ~(/name) | eq "ace")

:any/coalesce/keepsFalse
  |~~ False ends the walk as a value. ~~|
  ::law~({:on false} | coalesce ~(/on) ~(true) | not)

:any/coalesce/answersNullWhenEveryAlternativeFails
  |~~ When every alternative answers null or an error, the call answers null. ~~|
  ::law~({} | coalesce ~(/a) ~(/b) | eq null)

:any/coalesce/passesOverAFailure
  |~~ An error passes to the next alternative. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 2 :b 3} | coalesce ~(/missing) ~("fallback") | eq "fallback")

:any/coalesce/fallsToTheLast
  |~~ The last alternative answers when the rest give nothing. ~~|
  ::law~({:nickname null :name null}
    | coalesce ~(/nickname) ~(/name) ~("anonymous") | eq "anonymous")

:any/coalesce/takesAValueForAnAlternative
  |~~ A value stands for an alternative as its own answer. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 2} | coalesce ~(/missing) "fallback" | eq "fallback")

:any/coalesce/defaultsAMissingField
  |~~ A field missing or null takes the default. ~~|
  ::law~([{:t 45} {:t null} {}] | map ~(at :t | coalesce 30) | eq [45 30 30])

:any/coalesce/keepsAValue
  |~~ A value present stays. ~~|
  ::law~({:tags ["a"]} | /tags | coalesce [] | eq ["a"])

:any/coalesce/passesAnErrorBeforeIt
  |~~ An error the steps before answered passes over the call. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | /missing | coalesce 0 !| type | eq ::proj/KeyNotInMapError)

:any/coalesce/alternatives/EmptyError/refusesNoAlternative
  |~~ A coalesce of no alternative is refused. ~~|
  ::law~({} | coalesce
    !| [type /actualValue]
    |  eq [::any/coalesce/alternatives/EmptyError ::any/coalesce[]])

:any/coalesce/keepsZeroAndTheEmptyString
  |~~ Zero and the empty string end the walk. ~~|
  ::law~([0 ""] | map ~(coalesce 1) | eq [0 ""])

:any/cond
  |~~ Answers the branch of the first predicate that holds of the
      subject, the clauses alternating predicate and branch, each a
      quote run against the subject in its turn: `85 | cond ~(gte 90)
      ~("A") ~(gte 80) ~("B") ~("F")` answers `"B"`, so a chain of
      nested `if` flattens into one call. A clause left over after the
      pairs is the default branch; without one, a walk that matches
      nothing answers null. Each predicate answers a boolean, and one
      that answers another kind is refused at its place. Unlike the
      condition of `if`, a predicate is code, a quote, and one computed
      at the call, `(gte 90)`, is refused at its index. The call takes
      one pair at least.
      ::link~(::any/if | doc) ::link~(::any/coalesce | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/cond/answer
  |~~ The answer of the branch chosen, null when no predicate holds and
      no default is given.
  ~~|
  ::any

:any/cond/clauses
  |~~ The clauses, each a quote: predicates and their branches in turn,
      a last one alone the default.
  ~~|
  ~(/)

:any/cond/clauses/ElementNotQuoteError
  |~~ Each of the clauses is code, a quote, `~(gte 90)`: a predicate runs
      against the subject in its turn, so one computed at the call is
      refused at its index.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(every ~(is ::quote))

:any/cond/clauses/NoBranchesError
  |~~ `cond` takes its clauses in pairs, a predicate and its branch, a
      last clause alone the default; a call without one pair, a lone
      default among them, has nothing to answer.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(count | gte 2)

:any/cond/ConditionNotBooleanError
  |~~ Every predicate of `cond` answers a boolean; `:index` is the place
      in the args, from zero, of the predicate that answered another
      kind.
  ~~|

:any/cond/picksTheFirstThatHolds
  |~~ Eighty-five is a B. ~~|
  ::law~(85 | cond ~(gte 90) ~("A") ~(gte 80) ~("B") ~(gte 70) ~("C") ~("F") | eq "B")

:any/cond/readsTheSubject
  |~~ The predicates read the subject. ~~|
  ::law~({:role "admin"}
    | cond ~(/role | eq "admin") ~("full") ~(/role | eq "user") ~("limited")
    | eq "full")

:any/cond/fallsToTheDefault
  |~~ A score no predicate holds of takes the default. ~~|
  ::law~(65 | cond ~(gte 90) ~("A") ~(gte 80) ~("B") ~(gte 70) ~("C") ~("F") | eq "F")

:any/cond/answersNullWithoutADefault
  |~~ A walk that matches nothing without a default answers null. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | cond ~(gt 9) ~("big") | eq null)

:any/cond/clauses/ElementNotQuoteError/refusesAComputedPredicate
  |~~ A predicate computed at the call is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | cond (gt 3) ~("big")
    !| [type /index]
    |  eq [::any/cond/clauses/ElementNotQuoteError 0])

:any/cond/clauses/NoBranchesError/refusesNoPair
  |~~ A cond of no pair is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | cond !| type | eq ::any/cond/clauses/NoBranchesError)

:any/cond/clauses/NoBranchesError/refusesALoneDefault
  |~~ A lone default is no pair. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | cond ~("only") !| type | eq ::any/cond/clauses/NoBranchesError)

:any/cond/ConditionNotBooleanError/refusesANumberAnswer
  |~~ A predicate that answers a number is refused at its place. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | cond ~(1) ~("yes")
    !| [type /index /actualType]
    |  eq [::any/cond/ConditionNotBooleanError 0 ::number])

:any/apply
  |~~ Answers the code the call gives run against the subject,
      `5 | apply ~(add 1)` answering `6`. The code is a quote or a name
      that holds one, `apply double`, and its steps run in order, a
      leading `!|` routing the first through the fail track, so a
      pipeline suffix a `:trail` records runs again as it is. Code held
      as the subject runs on itself by `apply /`, and text runs once
      `parse` reads it, `"10 | add 3" | parse | apply /`. The code runs
      under the fork rule: its declarations stay inside it, and only its
      value comes out. A step written without its quote runs at the
      call, so `apply (add 1)` is refused, as is the error a step
      answered there. The call takes one code; code of several steps is
      one quote.
      ::link~(::quote | doc) ::link~(::string/parse | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/apply/answer
  |~~ The answer of the code run against the subject. ~~|
  ::any

:any/apply/code
  |~~ The code the call runs, a quote or a name that holds one. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:any/apply/code/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The code is a quote, `apply ~(add 1)`: a step written without its
      quote runs at the call, and its answer, an error among them, is no
      code.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:any/apply/code/NotGivenError
  |~~ `apply` runs the code the call gives, `apply ~(add 1)`; code held
      as the subject runs on itself by `apply /`.
  ~~|

:any/apply/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `apply` runs one code: steps run in order inside one quote,
      `apply ~(add 1 | mul 2)`.
  ~~|

:any/apply/runsTheCode
  |~~ The code runs against the subject. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | apply ~(add 1) | eq 6)

:any/apply/runsANameThatHoldsCode
  |~~ A name that holds a quote runs as the quote. ~~|
  ::law~(:double ~(mul 2) | 5 | apply double | eq 10)

:any/apply/runsStepsInOrder
  |~~ Several steps run in order. ~~|
  ::law~([[1 2] [3 4]] | apply ~(flat | sort) | eq [1 2 3 4])

:any/apply/runsParsedText
  |~~ Text parsed into a quote runs. ~~|
  ::law~("10 | add 3" | parse | apply / | eq 13)

:any/apply/keepsTheDeclarationsInside
  |~~ The declarations of the code stay inside it. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | apply ~(:x 1 | x) | :y / | env | has :x | not)

:any/apply/code/NotQuoteError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number computed at the call is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | apply 1 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::any/apply/code/NotQuoteError ::number])

:any/apply/code/NotQuoteError/refusesTheErrorOfAStep
  |~~ The error a step answered at the call is refused as no code. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | apply /nope
    !| [type /actualType]
    |  eq [::any/apply/code/NotQuoteError ::proj/SubjectNotProjectableError])

:any/apply/code/NotGivenError/refusesACallWithNoCode
  |~~ A call with no code is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(~(add 1) | apply !| type | eq ::any/apply/code/NotGivenError)

:any/apply/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesTwoCodes
  |~~ A call with two codes is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | apply ~(add 1) ~(mul 2) !| type | eq ::any/apply/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:any/apply/passesAValueByALeadingFailStep
  |~~ A leading `!|` passes a value on. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | apply ~(!| 0) | eq 5)

:any/apply/runsARecordedSuffixAgain
  |~~ The steps a trail skipped run again on a value. ~~|
  ::law~(1 | add "x" | mul 2 | add 3 !| (:rest /trail/0/skipped | 5 | apply rest) | eq 13)

:any/env
  |~~ Answers what the reader declared before the step, whatever the
      subject, the cells of a session before it among them: a map from
      each name to the record of its binding, `::binding` with its name,
      its docs, its value, its source and its origin. `:limit 10 | env`
      answers the record of `:limit`, and `env | /limit` hands it to the
      next step. A name with a path is held by its address. Inside a
      fork it holds the names before the fork and the fork's own, and
      the fork's stay inside it. What the language and the host declared
      stands apart, so at the start of a query it answers `{}`: `kinds`
      lists their kinds, and `manifest` what lies under a noun. The call
      takes no name.
      ::link~(::any/binding | doc) ::link~(::any/kinds | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/env/answer
  |~~ The records of what the reader declared, by name. ~~|
  ::map

:any/env/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `env` answers every name the reader declared and takes none: one
      record is read by its key, `env | /limit`.
  ~~|

:any/env/holdsADeclaredName
  |~~ A name declared before the step is held. ~~|
  ::law~(:limit 10 | env | has :limit)

:any/env/answersWhateverTheSubject
  |~~ The subject leaves the answer as it is. ~~|
  ::law~(:limit 10 | [1 2] | env | has :limit)

:any/env/holdsTheRecord
  |~~ Each name holds the record its declaration wrote. ~~|
  ::law~(:limit 10 | env | /limit | /value | eq 10)

:any/env/holdsANameWithAPath
  |~~ A name with a path is held by its address. ~~|
  ::law~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | :Box/area ~(1) | env | keys | eq #[:Box :Box/area])

:any/env/holdsTheNamesOfAFork
  |~~ Inside a fork the names before it and its own are held. ~~|
  ::law~(:a 1 | (:b 2 | env | keys) | eq #[:a :b])

:any/env/keepsTheNamesOfAForkInside
  |~~ The names of a fork stay inside it. ~~|
  ::law~(:a 1 | (:b 2) | env | keys | eq #[:a])

:any/env/isEmptyAtTheStart
  |~~ Nothing is declared at the start. ~~|
  ::law~(env | eq {})

:any/env/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesAName
  |~~ A name given to `env` is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~(:limit 10 | env :limit !| type | eq ::any/env/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:any/kinds
  |~~ Answers the set of the kinds the scope reads, each by its tag name,
      whatever the subject: those the language and the host declared,
      the topic of the language among them, and those the reader
      declares. A query asks which kinds there are before it asks one of
      them for its page, `::set | doc`, or for what lies under it,
      `::set | manifest`. A concept of the topic is no kind:
      `::qlang/values` is a page `doc` reads. The call takes no kind.
      ::link~(::any/env | doc) ::link~(::qlang | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/kinds/answer
  |~~ The tag names of the kinds the scope reads. ~~|
  ::set

:any/kinds/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `kinds` answers every kind and takes none: whether a kind is there
      is `kinds | has ::set`.
  ~~|

:any/kinds/holdsTheKindsOfTheLanguage
  |~~ The kinds of the language are read. ~~|
  ::law~(kinds | has ::vec)

:any/kinds/answersWhateverTheSubject
  |~~ The subject leaves the answer as it is. ~~|
  ::law~("text" | kinds | has ::vec)

:any/kinds/holdsTheTopic
  |~~ The topic of the language is a label too. ~~|
  ::law~(kinds | has ::qlang)

:any/kinds/holdsAReadersKind
  |~~ A kind the reader declares is read. ~~|
  ::law~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | kinds | has ::Box)

:any/kinds/holdsNoConcept
  |~~ A concept of the topic is no kind. ~~|
  ::law~(kinds | has ::qlang/values | not)

:any/kinds/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesAKind
  |~~ A kind given to `kinds` is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~(kinds ::set !| type | eq ::any/kinds/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:any/binding
  |~~ Answers the record of a name, what its declaration wrote into its
      scope: `::binding` with its `:name`, its `:docs`, its `:value`, its
      `:source` and its `:origin`, the source it was read from, so where
      a name was declared is read as data, `::vec/filter | binding |
      /origin` answering `:qlang/vec`. A word by its address reads the
      record its source declared, a name of the scope the record its
      declaration wrote, and any other value the record of its kind. The
      anchor, a keyword after it, reads the record of the member the
      subject holds under it, a part of a word or the word a value
      calls. The other axes project the record: `doc` its page, `source`
      its quote. A name nothing declares is refused, with the addresses
      where a word of that name lives.
      ::link~(::any/doc | doc) ::link~(::any/source | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/binding/answer
  |~~ The record of the name. ~~|
  ::binding

:any/binding/anchor
  |~~ The member read under the name, a keyword, null when the call gives
      none.
  ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:any/binding/anchor/NotKeywordOrNullError
  |~~ The anchor is a keyword or null, the last name of a name under the
      subject, `::vec | binding :sort`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::keyword ::null)

:any/binding/UnknownNameError
  |~~ `binding` found no declaration of the name it read: a keyword that
      names no binding of its scope, the last name of a word declared
      under a kind among them, or a tag name that names nothing. `:name`
      holds the name, and `:addresses` the addresses of the pages of
      that name, one for each kind that answers it, so the word is read
      by one of them, `::vec/filter | binding`. `source`, `doc`,
      `explain`, `laws` and `runLaws` read a name as `binding` does, and
      each is refused under a tag of its own, so the tag says which of
      them read it.
  ~~|

:any/binding/answersABinding
  |~~ The record is a `::binding`. ~~|
  ::law~(::vec/filter | binding | type | eq ::binding)

:any/binding/readsTheOriginOfAVerb
  |~~ A word's record names the source that declared it. ~~|
  ::law~(::vec/filter | binding | /origin | eq :qlang/vec)

:any/binding/readsTheValueOfAName
  |~~ A name's record holds its value. ~~|
  ::law~(:x 1 | :x | binding | /value | eq 1)

:any/binding/readsTheKindOfAValue
  |~~ A number reads the record of `::number`. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | binding | /name | eq :number)

:any/binding/readsAPartByAnAnchor
  |~~ An anchor reads the record of a part of a word. ~~|
  ::law~(::vec/take | binding :count | /name | eq :vec/take/count)

:any/binding/readsTheWordAValueCalls
  |~~ An anchor on a value reads the record of the word it calls. ~~|
  ::law~([1] | binding :count | /name | eq :vec/count)

:any/binding/holdsThePageDocReads
  |~~ The record holds the page `doc` reads. ~~|
  ::law~(:x |~~ The x. ~~| 1 | :x | binding | /docs | first | eq (:x | doc))

:any/binding/anchor/NotKeywordOrNullError/refusesANumber
  |~~ An anchor that is a number is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(::vec/take | binding 1 !| type | eq ::any/binding/anchor/NotKeywordOrNullError)

:any/binding/UnknownNameError/refusesANameNothingDeclares
  |~~ A name nothing declares is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(:nonexistent-binding | binding
    !| [type /name]
    |  eq [::any/binding/UnknownNameError :nonexistent-binding])

:any/binding/UnknownNameError/namesWhereTheWordLives
  |~~ A word's name is refused with the addresses where the word lives. ~~|
  ::law~(:filter | binding !| /addresses | has ::vec/filter)

:any/source
  |~~ Answers the code written under a name, one quote of its
      declarations in the order they were written, which `parse` prints
      as its text, a declaration a line: the declaration that binds the
      name in its scope and every declaration under its address, its
      parts, its answer, its refusals and its laws. `::Tag | source` reads what was written
      under the tag, a refusal its own declaration, and any other value
      the declaration of its kind. A binding no step declared, a value a
      host bound, answers null. Given a name, it reads the name the
      subject holds under it: for a tag name the name under its address,
      a part of the args of a word among them, and for any other subject
      the word the name calls after it. A name nothing declares is
      refused, with the addresses where a word of that name lives.
      ::link~(::any/doc | doc) ::link~(::any/binding | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/source/answer
  |~~ The code written under the name, null for a value a host bound. ~~|
  #[::quote ::null]

:any/source/name
  |~~ The name read under the subject, a keyword, null when the call
      gives none.
  ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:any/source/name/NotKeywordOrNullError
  |~~ The name is a keyword or null, the last name of a name under the
      subject, `::vec | source :sort`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::keyword ::null)

:any/source/UnknownNameError
  |~~ `source` found no declaration of the name it read, as `binding`
      finds none.
      ::link~(::any/binding/UnknownNameError | doc)
  ~~|

:any/source/readsEveryDeclarationUnderAName
  |~~ The source of a word holds the declarations of its parts. ~~|
  ::law~(::vec/take | source | map ~/name | has :vec/take/count)

:any/source/readsInTheOrderWritten
  |~~ The declarations under a name read in the order they were written. ~~|
  ::law~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | :Box/a ~(/0) | :Box/b ~(/1) | :Box/a/x ~(/0)
    | ::Box | source | map ~/name
    | eq [:Box :Box/a :Box/b :Box/a/x])

:any/source/printsTheDeclaration
  |~~ The source of a word prints as its declaration. ~~|
  ::law~(::vec/filter | source | parse | contains ":vec/filter")

:any/source/printsADeclarationALine
  |~~ The source of a word prints a declaration a line. ~~|
  ::law~(::number/round | source | parse | lines | first | eq ":number/round |~~ Answers the subject rounded to the digits after the point the call")

:any/source/printsARefusal
  |~~ A refusal's source prints as its declaration. ~~|
  ::law~(::number/NotFiniteError | source | parse | contains "NotFiniteError")

:any/source/readsTheKindOfAValue
  |~~ A string reads the source of `::string`. ~~|
  ::law~("text" | source | eq (::string | source))

:any/source/readsAPartByAnAnchor
  |~~ A name reads the source of a part. ~~|
  ::law~(::vec/take | source :count | eq (::vec/take/count | source))

:any/source/readsTheWordAValueCalls
  |~~ A name on a value reads the source of the word it calls. ~~|
  ::law~([1] | source :count | eq (::vec/count | source))

:any/source/name/NotKeywordOrNullError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A name that is a number is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(::vec/take | source 1 !| type | eq ::any/source/name/NotKeywordOrNullError)

:any/source/UnknownNameError/refusesANameNothingDeclares
  |~~ A name nothing declares is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(:nonexistent-binding | source
    !| [type /name]
    |  eq [::any/source/UnknownNameError :nonexistent-binding])

:any/source/UnknownNameError/namesWhereTheWordLives
  |~~ A word's name is refused with the addresses where the word lives. ~~|
  ::law~(:filter | source !| /addresses | has ::vec/filter)

:any/doc
  |~~ Answers the page of a name, the passage from an address into its
      description: `::vec/filter | doc` the page of a word by its
      address, `:limit | doc` the page of the declaration that binds
      `:limit` in its scope, null for a declaration without one. A page
      answers its text by `content`, its links by `links` and its
      snippets by `snippets`, and the laws of the name stand under it,
      `::vec/take | laws`. Any other value reads the page of its kind,
      and the `:kind` field of a map names nothing. The anchor, a keyword
      after `doc`, reads the page of the member the subject holds under
      it, the word a value calls or a part of a word; a vector of
      anchors walks a path, each read within the page the one before
      reached, and a set of anchors reads a page at each. A name nothing
      declares is refused, with the addresses where a word of that name
      lives, as is an anchor no member answers.
      ::link~(::any/laws | doc) ::link~(::doc | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/doc/answer
  |~~ The page of the name, null for a declaration without one, a vector
      of pages for a set of anchors.
  ~~|
  #[::doc ::null ::vec]

:any/doc/anchor
  |~~ The anchor within the page: a keyword, a vector of them that walks
      a path or a set of them, null when the call gives none.
  ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:any/doc/anchor/NotKeywordOrVecOrSetOrNullError
  |~~ The anchor is a keyword, a vector, a set or null, the last names of
      names under the subject, `::qlang | doc :pipeline`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::keyword ::vec ::set ::null)

:any/doc/anchor/ElementNotKeywordError
  |~~ An anchor of the path `doc` reads is no keyword: each step of the
      path names a member by a keyword, `doc [:take :count]`.
  ~~|

:any/doc/UnknownNameError
  |~~ `doc` found no declaration of the name it read, as `binding` finds
      none, or no member of the page before it under an anchor of its
      path.
      ::link~(::any/binding/UnknownNameError | doc)
  ~~|

:any/doc/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `doc` reads one anchor: a path of anchors is a vector,
      `doc [:take :count]`, and the pages of several a set,
      `doc #[:take :drop]`.
  ~~|

:any/doc/readsThePageOfAVerb
  |~~ The page of `filter` speaks of its predicate. ~~|
  ::law~(::vec/filter | doc | content | contains "predicate")

:any/doc/answersNullForADeclarationWithoutAPage
  |~~ A declaration without a page answers null. ~~|
  ::law~(:x 1 | :x | doc | eq null)

:any/doc/readsThePageOfARefusal
  |~~ A refusal's page is a doc. ~~|
  ::law~(::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError | doc | type | eq ::doc)

:any/doc/readsThePageOfAKind
  |~~ A number reads the page of `::number`. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | doc | eq (::number | doc))

:any/doc/readsNoKindFromAField
  |~~ A map reads the page of maps, whatever its `:kind` says. ~~|
  ::law~({:kind ::set} | doc | eq (::map | doc))

:any/doc/readsAMemberByAnAnchor
  |~~ An anchor reads the page of a member. ~~|
  ::law~(::map | doc :minus | eq (::map/minus | doc))

:any/doc/readsTheWordAValueCalls
  |~~ An anchor on a value reads the page of the word it calls. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | doc :count | eq (::vec/count | doc))

:any/doc/readsAPartOfAWord
  |~~ An anchor reads the page of a part of a word. ~~|
  ::law~(:any/f ~(add n) | :any/f/n |~~ how many ~~| ~(/0)
    | ::any/f | doc :n | content | eq " how many ")

:any/doc/walksAPath
  |~~ A vector of anchors walks a path. ~~|
  ::law~(::vec | doc [:take] | eq (::vec/take | doc))

:any/doc/readsAPageAtEachAnchor
  |~~ A set of anchors reads a page at each. ~~|
  ::law~(::qlang | doc #[:pipeline :errors] | count | eq 2)

:any/doc/anchor/NotKeywordOrVecOrSetOrNullError/refusesANumber
  |~~ An anchor that is a number is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(::vec | doc 5 !| type | eq ::any/doc/anchor/NotKeywordOrVecOrSetOrNullError)

:any/doc/UnknownNameError/refusesANameNothingDeclares
  |~~ A name nothing declares is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(:nonexistent-binding | doc
    !| [type /name]
    |  eq [::any/doc/UnknownNameError :nonexistent-binding])

:any/doc/UnknownNameError/namesWhereTheWordLives
  |~~ A word's name is refused with the addresses where the word lives. ~~|
  ::law~(:filter | doc !| /addresses | has ::vec/filter)

:any/doc/UnknownNameError/namesWhereAnotherWordLives
  |~~ Another word's name is refused with its addresses. ~~|
  ::law~(:reduce | doc !| /addresses | has ::vec/reduce)

:any/doc/UnknownNameError/refusesAnAnchorNoMemberAnswers
  |~~ An anchor no member answers is refused with the name it read. ~~|
  ::law~(::vec/take | doc :nope !| [type /name] | eq [::any/doc/UnknownNameError :nope])

:any/doc/anchor/ElementNotKeywordError/refusesANumberInThePath
  |~~ A number in the path is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~(::vec | doc [:take 1] !| [type /index] | eq [::any/doc/anchor/ElementNotKeywordError 1])

:any/doc/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesTwoAnchors
  |~~ Two anchors given apart are refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~(::vec | doc :take :count !| type | eq ::any/doc/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:any/explain
  |~~ Answers the explanation of the subject, `::explanation{:page …
      :value …}`: the page `doc` reads for it, then the value itself, so
      it prints as the page above what it explains. A name reads its own
      page, a value the page of its kind, and an error `!|` opened the
      page of the refusal it carries, which says what failed and how the
      call is repaired. A name nothing declares is refused as `binding`
      refuses it. The call takes no anchor: the page of a member is read
      by `doc`.
      ::link~(::any/doc | doc) ::link~(::explanation | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/explain/answer
  |~~ The page above the value it explains. ~~|
  ::explanation

:any/explain/UnknownNameError
  |~~ `explain` found no declaration of the page of its subject, as
      `binding` finds none.
      ::link~(::any/binding/UnknownNameError | doc)
  ~~|

:any/explain/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `explain` explains its subject and takes no anchor: the page of a
      member is read by `doc`, `[1 2] | doc :count`, and the member is
      explained by its address, `::vec/count | explain`.
  ~~|

:any/explain/explainsAValueByItsKind
  |~~ A number is explained by the page of its kind. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | explain | page | eq (::number | doc))

:any/explain/holdsThePageAndTheValue
  |~~ The explanation holds the page `doc` reads, then the value it explains. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | explain | [(page | type) value] | eq [::doc 42])

:any/explain/explainsAWordByItsPage
  |~~ A word is explained by its page. ~~|
  ::law~(::number/add | explain | page | content | contains "plus the number")

:any/explain/explainsAnErrorByItsSite
  |~~ An error is explained by the page of the refusal it carries. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | add 1 !| explain | page | content | contains "adds to a number")

:any/explain/UnknownNameError/refusesANameNothingDeclares
  |~~ A name nothing declares is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(:nonexistent-binding | explain
    !| [type /name]
    |  eq [::any/explain/UnknownNameError :nonexistent-binding])

:any/explain/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesAnAnchor
  |~~ An anchor given to `explain` is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | explain :count !| type | eq ::any/explain/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:any/laws
  |~~ Answers the laws declared under the name the subject reads, as
      `doc` reads it, in the order of their declarations: a map from
      each law's name, which says the claim it proves, to the pair of its
      page, the sentence of the claim, and its query, which answers
      true. A value reads the laws of its kind, and a name with no laws
      answers `{}`. `runLaws` runs them, and `tutorial` pairs them with
      the page. A name nothing declares is refused as `binding` refuses
      it. The call takes no anchor: the laws of a member are read by its
      address.
      ::link~(::any/runLaws | doc) ::link~(::any/tutorial | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/laws/answer
  |~~ The name of each law and the pair of its page and its query. ~~|
  ::map

:any/laws/UnknownNameError
  |~~ `laws` found no declaration of the name it read, as `binding`
      finds none.
      ::link~(::any/binding/UnknownNameError | doc)
  ~~|

:any/laws/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `laws` reads the laws of its subject and takes no anchor: the laws
      of a member are read by its address, `::vec/take | laws`.
  ~~|

:any/laws/answersEachLawByItsName
  |~~ A law declared under a name is answered under its own name. ~~|
  ::law~(:any/k ~(5) | :any/k/answersFive |~~ k answers five. ~~| ::law~(0 | k | eq 5)
    | ::any/k | laws | keys | eq #[:answersFive])

:any/laws/pairsThePageWithTheQuery
  |~~ Each law is the pair of its page and its query. ~~|
  ::law~(:any/k ~(5) | :any/k/answersFive |~~ k answers five. ~~| ::law~(0 | k | eq 5)
    | ::any/k | laws | /answersFive | [(/0 | content) /1]
    | eq [" k answers five. " ~(0 | k | eq 5)])

:any/laws/readsTheLawsOfTheKindOfAValue
  |~~ A value reads the laws of its kind. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | laws | eq (::number | laws))

:any/laws/answersNoLawsAsAnEmptyMap
  |~~ A name with no laws answers an empty map. ~~|
  ::law~(:any/k ~(5) | ::any/k | laws | eq {})

:any/laws/UnknownNameError/refusesANameNothingDeclares
  |~~ A name nothing declares is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(:nonexistent-binding | laws
    !| [type /name]
    |  eq [::any/laws/UnknownNameError :nonexistent-binding])

:any/laws/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesAnAnchor
  |~~ An anchor given to `laws` is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~(::vec | laws :take !| type | eq ::any/laws/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:any/runLaws
  |~~ Answers the results of the laws under the name the subject reads,
      as `doc` reads it, a word by its address among them, one
      `{:law :says :actual :ok :error}` per law in their order: `:law`
      its address, `:says` its page, the claim it proves, `:actual` what
      its query answered, null where it answered an error, which `:error`
      holds as the error at rest, and `:ok` whether the answer is true,
      so a law that answers false or an error fails. A value runs the
      laws of its kind, and a name of the scope the laws declared under
      it. The anchor, a keyword after it, runs the laws of the member the
      name holds under it, a concept of the language among them. A name
      nothing declares is refused as `binding` refuses it.
      ::link~(::any/laws | doc) ::link~(::any/tutorial | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/runLaws/answer
  |~~ A map for each law: the law, its claim, what it answered and
      whether it held.
  ~~|
  ::vec

:any/runLaws/anchor
  |~~ The member whose laws run, a keyword, null when the call gives
      none.
  ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:any/runLaws/anchor/NotKeywordOrNullError
  |~~ The anchor is a keyword or null, the last name of a name under the
      subject, `::vec | runLaws :sort`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::keyword ::null)

:any/runLaws/UnknownNameError
  |~~ `runLaws` found no declaration of the name it read, as `binding`
      finds none.
      ::link~(::any/binding/UnknownNameError | doc)
  ~~|

:any/runLaws/runsTheLawsOfAVerb
  |~~ The first law of `add` holds. ~~|
  ::law~(::number/add | runLaws | first | /ok)

:any/runLaws/holdsEveryLawOfAdd
  |~~ Every law of `add` holds. ~~|
  ::law~(::number/add | runLaws | map ~/ok | distinct | eq #[true])

:any/runLaws/failsALawThatAnswersFalse
  |~~ A law that answers false fails. ~~|
  ::law~(:any/k ~(5) | :any/k/wrong |~~ One is two. ~~| ::law~(1 | eq 2)
    | ::any/k | runLaws | first | /ok | not)

:any/runLaws/runsTheLawsOfTheKindOfAValue
  |~~ A value runs the laws of its kind, as `doc` reads its page. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | runLaws | count | eq (::number | runLaws | count))

:any/runLaws/runsTheLawsUnderTheName
  |~~ The laws under the name a keyword reads run. ~~|
  ::law~(:limit |~~ A limit. ~~| 10 | :limit/holds |~~ It holds. ~~| ::law~(true)
    | :limit | runLaws | map ~/ok | eq [true])

:any/runLaws/runsTheLawsUnderAnAnchor
  |~~ An anchor runs the laws of the name under the one read. ~~|
  ::law~(::qlang | runLaws :values | every ~(/ok))

:any/runLaws/anchor/NotKeywordOrNullError/refusesANumber
  |~~ An anchor that is a number is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(::qlang | runLaws 1 !| type | eq ::any/runLaws/anchor/NotKeywordOrNullError)

:any/runLaws/UnknownNameError/refusesANameNothingDeclares
  |~~ A name nothing declares is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(:nonexistent-binding | runLaws
    !| [type /name]
    |  eq [::any/runLaws/UnknownNameError :nonexistent-binding])

:any/runLaws/UnknownNameError/refusesATagNothingDeclares
  |~~ A tag nothing declares is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(::Nonexistent | runLaws
    !| [type /name]
    |  eq [::any/runLaws/UnknownNameError ::Nonexistent])

:any/runLaws/UnknownNameError/namesWhereTheWordLives
  |~~ A word's name is refused with the addresses where the word lives. ~~|
  ::law~(:filter | runLaws !| /addresses | has ::vec/filter)

:any/runLaws/failsALawThatAnswersAFailure
  |~~ A law that answers an error fails, and `:error` holds it at rest. ~~|
  ::law~(:any/k ~(5) | :any/k/bad |~~ b ~~| ::law~(add "x") | ::any/k | runLaws | first
    | [(/ok) (/actual) (/error | type)] | eq [false null ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError])

:any/tutorial
  |~~ Answers the page of the name the subject reads and its laws, the
      pair a reader learns the name from: the prose that says what it
      is, and the laws that show it at work, each the pair of its claim
      and its query, `::number | tutorial`. A value reads the page and
      the laws of its kind. A name nothing declares is refused as `doc`
      refuses it, under the tag of `doc`. The call takes no anchor: a
      member is read by its address, `::vec/take | tutorial`.
      ::link~(::any/doc | doc) ::link~(::any/laws | doc)
  ~~|
  ~(:subject / | doc | [/ (subject | laws)])

:any/tutorial/answer
  |~~ The page and the laws. ~~|
  ::vec

:any/tutorial/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `tutorial` reads the name of its subject and takes no anchor: a
      member is read by its address, `::vec/take | tutorial`.
  ~~|

:any/tutorial/pairsThePageWithTheLaws
  |~~ A tutorial is the page of a name and its laws. ~~|
  ::law~(::number | tutorial | [(/0 | type) (/1 | type)] | eq [::doc ::map])

:any/tutorial/readsTheKindOfAValue
  |~~ A value reads the page and the laws of its kind. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | tutorial | eq (::number | tutorial))

:any/tutorial/refusesANameNothingDeclares
  |~~ A name nothing declares is refused as `doc` refuses it. ~~|
  ::law~(:nothere | tutorial !| type | eq ::any/doc/UnknownNameError)

:any/tutorial/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesAnAnchor
  |~~ An anchor given to `tutorial` is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~(::vec | tutorial :take !| type | eq ::any/tutorial/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:any/tag
  |~~ Answers the subject under the name the call gives, a tag name:
      `5 | tag ::Box` answers `::Box(5)`, a value minted at run time as
      the literal `::Box(5)` mints it where it is written, its type the
      tag and its payload the subject. Given no name, it reads the pair
      `[type payload]` answers, `[::Box 5] | tag`, so a value split tags
      back into itself. A tag of a kind mints through the kind:
      `5 | tag ::number` answers `5`, a payload the kind refuses is
      refused, `"5" | tag ::number`, and a kind the reader declares
      holds its invariants. A tag over a tagged value nests it;
      replacing the tag goes through the payload,
      `payload | tag ::Crate`. A tag over an error at rest names its
      site, `[::Late !{:by 3}] | tag` answering `::Late!{:by 3}`, and
      `raise` raises it under that tag.
      The call takes one name; a keyword, `:box`, names a field and no
      kind.
      ::link~(::any/type | doc) ::link~(::tagged/payload | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/tag/answer
  |~~ The subject under the tag, read by its kind. ~~|
  ::any

:any/tag/name
  |~~ The tag laid over the subject, null when the call gives none and
      the subject is a pair.
  ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:any/tag/name/NotTagOrNullError
  |~~ The name is a tag name, `::Box`; a keyword, `:box`, names a field
      and no kind.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::tag ::null)

:any/tag/PairTagNotTagError
  |~~ The tag of the pair `tag` reads is a tag name, `[::Box 5] | tag`; a
      keyword, `:box`, names a field and no kind.
  ~~|

:any/tag/SubjectNotPairError
  |~~ `tag` alone reads a pair, `[::Box value] | tag`, the tag and the
      value it goes over; a subject of another shape is refused, a
      vector of another length among them.
  ~~|

:any/tag/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `tag` lays one tag: two tags nest one call after the other,
      `tag ::Box | tag ::Crate`, and a pair whose value comes first
      turns before it is read, `[/1 /0] | tag`.
  ~~|

:any/tag/standsOverTheValue
  |~~ The payload of a tagged number is the number. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | tag ::Box | payload | eq 42)

:any/tag/namesTheKind
  |~~ The tag is the type of the value. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | tag ::Triple | type | eq ::Triple)

:any/tag/readsAPair
  |~~ Given no name, `tag` reads a pair. ~~|
  ::law~([::Box 5] | tag | eq ::Box(5))

:any/tag/tagsASplitBack
  |~~ A split value tags back into itself. ~~|
  ::law~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | ::Box[1 2 3] | [type payload] | tag | eq ::Box[1 2 3])

:any/tag/mintsThroughTheKind
  |~~ A tag of a kind of the core reads the payload as the kind. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | tag ::number | eq 5)

:any/tag/refusesAPayloadTheKindRefuses
  |~~ A payload the kind refuses is refused by the kind. ~~|
  ::law~("5" | tag ::number !| type | eq ::number/PayloadNotNumberError)

:any/tag/holdsTheInvariantsOfAReadersKind
  |~~ A kind the reader declares holds its invariants. ~~|
  ::law~(:Pos |~~ A positive number. ~~| | :Pos/NotPositiveError ::holds~(gt 0)
    | -3 | tag ::Pos
    !| type
    |  eq ::Pos/NotPositiveError)

:any/tag/nestsATaggedValue
  |~~ A tag over a tagged value nests it. ~~|
  ::law~(:Inner |~~ An inner kind. ~~| | :Outer |~~ An outer kind. ~~|
    | ::Inner[1] | tag ::Outer | payload | type | eq ::Inner)

:any/tag/putsAnotherTagOverThePayload
  |~~ Another tag goes over the payload taken out. ~~|
  ::law~(::Box(1) | payload | tag ::Crate | eq ::Crate(1))

:any/tag/namesTheSiteOfAnErrorAtRest
  |~~ A tag over an error at rest names its site. ~~|
  ::law~([::Late !{:by 3}] | tag | eq ::Late!{:by 3})

:any/tag/raisesAnOpenedErrorAsItself
  |~~ An opened error split and tagged back raises as itself. ~~|
  ::law~(1 | add "1" !| [type payload] | tag | raise
    !| type
    |  eq ::number/add/addend/NotNumberError)

:any/tag/name/NotTagOrNullError/refusesAKeyword
  |~~ A keyword given as the name is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | tag :box !| [type /actualType] | eq [::any/tag/name/NotTagOrNullError ::keyword])

:any/tag/PairTagNotTagError/refusesAKeyword
  |~~ A keyword names a field and no kind. ~~|
  ::law~([:foo 42] | tag !| [type /actualType] | eq [::any/tag/PairTagNotTagError ::keyword])

:any/tag/SubjectNotPairError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number is no pair. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | tag !| [type /actualType] | eq [::any/tag/SubjectNotPairError ::number])

:any/tag/SubjectNotPairError/refusesAVectorOfThree
  |~~ A vector of three is no pair. ~~|
  ::law~([::Foo 1 2] | tag !| [type /actualType] | eq [::any/tag/SubjectNotPairError ::vec])

:any/tag/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesTwoTags
  |~~ Two tags given to one call are refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | tag ::Box ::Crate !| type | eq ::any/tag/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:any/sumOf
  |~~ Answers the sum of the numbers the call computes against the
      subject, as many as it gives, the subject only what they are read
      from: two fields of one record add by `sumOf /x /y`,
      `{:x 10 :y 3} | sumOf /x /y` answering `13`. A field the record
      lacks answers the error of `/`, and one that may be missing is
      read with its default, `(at :y | coalesce 0)`. Text that holds a
      number is read first by `parseJson`, and the numbers of a vector
      sum by `sum`. The call gives one number at least.
      ::link~(::number/add | doc) ::link~(::vec/sum | doc)
  ~~|
  ~(numbers | sum)

:any/sumOf/answer
  |~~ The sum of the numbers. ~~|
  ::number

:any/sumOf/numbers
  |~~ The numbers to add, one at least. ~~|
  ~(/)

:any/sumOf/numbers/EmptyError
  |~~ `sumOf` adds the numbers the call gives, one at least,
      `sumOf /x /y`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(count | gt 0)

:any/sumOf/numbers/ElementNotNumberError
  |~~ Each of the numbers is a number; text that holds one is read by
      `parseJson`, `sumOf (/x | parseJson) /y`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(every ~(is ::number))

:any/sumOf/addsTwoFields
  |~~ Two fields of one map add. ~~|
  ::law~({:x 10 :y 3} | sumOf /x /y | eq 13)

:any/sumOf/addsAsManyAsGiven
  |~~ As many fields as the call gives add. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | sumOf /a /b /c | eq 6)

:any/sumOf/answersTheErrorOfAMissingField
  |~~ A field the record lacks answers the error of `/`. ~~|
  ::law~({:x 1} | sumOf /x /y !| type | eq ::proj/KeyNotInMapError)

:any/sumOf/readsAMissingFieldByItsDefault
  |~~ A field that may be missing is read with its default. ~~|
  ::law~({:x 1} | sumOf /x (at :y | coalesce 0) | eq 1)

:any/sumOf/readsTextByParseJson
  |~~ Text that holds a number is read first by `parseJson`. ~~|
  ::law~({:x "1" :y 2} | sumOf (/x | parseJson) /y | eq 3)

:any/sumOf/numbers/EmptyError/refusesNoNumber
  |~~ A call with no number is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~({} | sumOf !| type | eq ::any/sumOf/numbers/EmptyError)

:any/sumOf/numbers/ElementNotNumberError/refusesText
  |~~ Text is no number, and is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~({:x "1"} | sumOf /x
    !| [type /index]
    |  eq [::any/sumOf/numbers/ElementNotNumberError 0])

:any/mulOf
  |~~ Answers the product of the factors the call computes against the
      subject, as many as it gives: the price and the quantity of one
      line multiply by `mulOf /price /qty`, `{:price 4 :qty 3} | mulOf
      /price /qty` answering `12`. One factor answers itself, and the
      call gives one at least. Text that holds a number is read first by
      `parseJson`.
      ::link~(::number/mul | doc) ::link~(::any/sumOf | doc)
  ~~|
  ~(factors | reduce 1 ~(mul))

:any/mulOf/answer
  |~~ The factors multiplied. ~~|
  ::number

:any/mulOf/factors
  |~~ The numbers multiplied, one at least. ~~|
  ~(/)

:any/mulOf/factors/EmptyError
  |~~ `mulOf` multiplies the factors the call gives, one at least,
      `mulOf /price /qty`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(count | gt 0)

:any/mulOf/factors/ElementNotNumberError
  |~~ Each of the factors is a number; text that holds one is read by
      `parseJson`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(every ~(is ::number))

:any/mulOf/multipliesAsManyAsGiven
  |~~ As many factors as the call gives multiply. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 2 :b 3 :c 4} | mulOf /a /b /c | eq 24)

:any/mulOf/answersOneFactor
  |~~ One factor answers itself. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 7} | mulOf /a | eq 7)

:any/mulOf/factors/EmptyError/refusesNoFactor
  |~~ A call with no factor is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 2} | mulOf !| type | eq ::any/mulOf/factors/EmptyError)

:any/mulOf/factors/ElementNotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A string is no factor, and is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 2} | mulOf /a "x"
    !| [type /index]
    |  eq [::any/mulOf/factors/ElementNotNumberError 1])

:any/mulOf/multipliesTwoFields
  |~~ The price and the quantity of one line multiply. ~~|
  ::law~({:price 4 :qty 3} | mulOf /price /qty | eq 12)

:any/subOf
  |~~ Answers the minuend the call computes against the subject less the
      subtrahend: what is left to pay is `subOf /total /paid`,
      `{:total 10 :paid 3} | subOf /total /paid` answering `7`. The call
      gives the two, the minuend first; a sum subtracted is computed in
      its place, `subOf /total (sumOf /a /b)`.
      ::link~(::number/sub | doc) ::link~(::any/sumOf | doc)
  ~~|
  ~(minuend | sub subtrahend)

:any/subOf/answer
  |~~ The minuend less the subtrahend. ~~|
  ::number

:any/subOf/minuend
  |~~ The number subtracted from. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:any/subOf/minuend/NotNumberError
  |~~ The minuend is a number, `{:a 5 :b 3} | subOf /a /b`; text that holds
      one is read by `parseJson` first, `subOf (/a | parseJson) /b`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:any/subOf/subtrahend
  |~~ The number subtracted. ~~|
  ~(/1)

:any/subOf/subtrahend/NotNumberError
  |~~ The subtrahend is a number, `{:a 5 :b 3} | subOf /a /b`; text that
      holds one is read by `parseJson` first, `subOf /a (/b | parseJson)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:any/subOf/subtrahend/NotGivenError
  |~~ `subOf` subtracts the subtrahend from the minuend and was given the
      minuend alone: `subOf /total /paid`.
  ~~|

:any/subOf/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `subOf` subtracts one number from another: a sum subtracted is
      computed in its place, `subOf /total (sumOf /a /b)`.
  ~~|

:any/subOf/subtractsASumInItsPlace
  |~~ A sum subtracted is computed in its place. ~~|
  ::law~({:total 10 :a 3 :b 2} | subOf /total (sumOf /a /b) | eq 5)

:any/subOf/minuend/NotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A minuend that is a string is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~({:t "a" :p 1} | subOf /t /p
    !| [type /actualType]
    |  eq [::any/subOf/minuend/NotNumberError ::string])

:any/subOf/subtrahend/NotGivenError/refusesOneNumber
  |~~ A call with the minuend alone is refused under the subtrahend. ~~|
  ::law~({:t 10} | subOf /t !| type | eq ::any/subOf/subtrahend/NotGivenError)

:any/subOf/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesAThirdNumber
  |~~ A third number is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~({:t 10 :p 3 :q 1} | subOf /t /p /q !| type | eq ::any/subOf/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:any/subOf/subtrahend/NotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A subtrahend that is no number is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 5 :b "3"} | subOf /a /b !| type | eq ::any/subOf/subtrahend/NotNumberError)

:any/subOf/subtractsTwoFields
  |~~ What is left to pay is the total less what was paid. ~~|
  ::law~({:total 10 :paid 3} | subOf /total /paid | eq 7)

:any/divOf
  |~~ Answers the dividend the call computes against the subject divided
      by the divisor: a mean is `divOf /sum /count`,
      `{:sum 20 :count 4} | divOf /sum /count` answering `5`. A quotient
      that falls between whole numbers is a decimal, and a divisor of
      zero is refused as `div` refuses it. The call gives the two, the
      dividend first; a product divided is computed in its place,
      `divOf /total (mulOf /a /b)`.
      ::link~(::number/div | doc) ::link~(::any/mulOf | doc)
  ~~|
  ~(dividend | div divisor)

:any/divOf/answer
  |~~ The dividend divided by the divisor. ~~|
  ::number

:any/divOf/dividend
  |~~ The number divided. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:any/divOf/dividend/NotNumberError
  |~~ The dividend is a number, `{:a 6 :b 3} | divOf /a /b`; text that holds
      one is read by `parseJson` first, `divOf (/a | parseJson) /b`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:any/divOf/divisor
  |~~ The number divided by. ~~|
  ~(/1)

:any/divOf/divisor/NotNumberError
  |~~ The divisor is a number, `{:a 6 :b 3} | divOf /a /b`; text that holds
      one is read by `parseJson` first, `divOf /a (/b | parseJson)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:any/divOf/divisor/NotGivenError
  |~~ `divOf` divides the dividend by the divisor and was given the
      dividend alone: `divOf /sum /count`.
  ~~|

:any/divOf/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `divOf` divides one number by another: a product divided is
      computed in its place, `divOf /total (mulOf /a /b)`.
  ~~|

:any/divOf/dividesByTheSecond
  |~~ A mean is the sum over the count. ~~|
  ::law~({:sum 20 :count 4} | divOf /sum /count | eq 5)

:any/divOf/answersADecimal
  |~~ A quotient between whole numbers is a decimal. ~~|
  ::law~({:sum 7 :count 2} | divOf /sum /count | eq 3.5)

:any/divOf/refusesADivisorOfZero
  |~~ A divisor of zero is refused as `div` refuses it. ~~|
  ::law~({:sum 20 :count 0} | divOf /sum /count !| type | eq ::number/div/divisor/ZeroError)

:any/divOf/divisor/NotGivenError/refusesOneNumber
  |~~ A call with the dividend alone is refused under the divisor. ~~|
  ::law~({:s 20} | divOf /s !| type | eq ::any/divOf/divisor/NotGivenError)

:any/divOf/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesAThirdNumber
  |~~ A third number is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~({:s 20 :c 4 :x 1} | divOf /s /c /x !| type | eq ::any/divOf/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:any/divOf/dividend/NotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A dividend that is no number is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~({:a "6" :b 3} | divOf /a /b !| type | eq ::any/divOf/dividend/NotNumberError)

:any/divOf/divisor/NotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A divisor that is no number is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 6 :b "3"} | divOf /a /b !| type | eq ::any/divOf/divisor/NotNumberError)

:any/allOf
  |~~ Answers whether every condition the call gives holds of the
      subject: two conditions of one record hold together by
      `allOf /active (/age | gte 18)`. Each modifier is a condition
      computed at the call, every one of them, or a quote that answers
      one in its turn, run on the subject once every condition before it
      held: a condition that fails where an earlier one does not hold is
      written as such a quote, `allOf (/email | ne null) ~(/email |
      endsWith ".org")`, and the first condition that does not hold
      answers `false` with the quotes after it unrun; a quote that fails
      answers its error. The call gives one condition at least.
      ::link~(::any/anyOf | doc) ::link~(::vec/every | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/allOf/answer
  |~~ Whether every condition holds. ~~|
  ::boolean

:any/allOf/conditions
  |~~ The conditions, booleans and quotes that answer them, one at least. ~~|
  ~(/)

:any/allOf/conditions/EmptyError
  |~~ `allOf` asks whether every condition the call gives holds, one at
      least, `allOf /active (/age | gte 18)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(count | gt 0)

:any/allOf/conditions/ElementNotBooleanOrQuoteError
  |~~ Each modifier of `allOf` is a condition computed at the call,
      `(/age | gte 18)`, or a quote that answers one in its turn,
      `~(/email | endsWith ".org")`. A condition computed at the call is
      computed at every place, the first among them, and one that
      answered an error is refused here, the error its value: a field a
      record may lack is read softly, `allOf (at :b | eq 1)`, and a
      condition that fails where an earlier one does not hold is written
      as a quote that answers it in its turn, which runs only once every
      condition before it held.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(every ~(is ::boolean ::quote))

:any/allOf/ConditionNotBooleanError
  |~~ A quote among the conditions of `allOf` answers a boolean when it
      runs; `:index` names, from zero, the condition whose quote answered
      another kind, and a quote that reads a field compares it to answer
      a condition, `~(/email | ne null)`.
  ~~|

:any/allOf/holdsWhenEveryConditionHolds
  |~~ An active adult passes both conditions. ~~|
  ::law~({:active true :age 25} | allOf /active (/age | gte 18))

:any/allOf/failsWhenOneFails
  |~~ A minor fails the second. ~~|
  ::law~({:active true :age 15} | allOf /active (/age | gte 18) | not)

:any/allOf/runsAQuoteInItsTurn
  |~~ A quote runs once the conditions before it held. ~~|
  ::law~([{:email null} {:email "a@b.org"}]
    | map ~(allOf (/email | ne null) ~(/email | endsWith ".org"))
    | eq [false true])

:any/allOf/answersTheFailureOfAQuote
  |~~ A quote that fails answers its error. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | allOf ~(/b | eq 1) !| [type /key] | eq [::proj/KeyNotInMapError :b])

:any/allOf/conditions/ElementNotBooleanOrQuoteError/refusesAFieldMissingAtTheFirstPlace
  |~~ A field the record lacks is refused at its place, the first among them. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | allOf (/b | eq 1)
    !| [type /index /actualType]
    |  eq [::any/allOf/conditions/ElementNotBooleanOrQuoteError 0 ::proj/KeyNotInMapError])

:any/allOf/conditions/ElementNotBooleanOrQuoteError/readsAMissingFieldSoftly
  |~~ A field the record may lack is read softly. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | allOf (at :b | eq 1) | not)

:any/allOf/conditions/ElementNotBooleanOrQuoteError/refusesAConditionThatFailedAtTheCall
  |~~ A condition computed at the call is computed even where an earlier one does not hold, and its error is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~({:age 15} | allOf (/age | gte 18) /licence
    !| [type /index /actualType]
    |  eq [::any/allOf/conditions/ElementNotBooleanOrQuoteError 1 ::proj/KeyNotInMapError])

:any/allOf/conditions/ElementNotBooleanOrQuoteError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number is no condition, and is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~({:age 15} | allOf /age
    !| [type /index /actualType]
    |  eq [::any/allOf/conditions/ElementNotBooleanOrQuoteError 0 ::number])

:any/allOf/ConditionNotBooleanError/refusesAQuoteThatAnswersAString
  |~~ A quote that answers a string is refused at its place. ~~|
  ::law~({:email "a@b.org"} | allOf true ~(/email)
    !| [type /index /actualType]
    |  eq [::any/allOf/ConditionNotBooleanError 1 ::string])

:any/allOf/ConditionNotBooleanError/holdsAQuoteThatCompares
  |~~ A quote that compares the field it reads answers a condition. ~~|
  ::law~({:email "a@b.org"} | allOf true ~(/email | ne null))

:any/allOf/conditions/EmptyError/refusesNoCondition
  |~~ A call with no condition is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~({} | allOf !| type | eq ::any/allOf/conditions/EmptyError)

:any/anyOf
  |~~ Answers whether some condition the call gives holds of the
      subject: a high score passes without the flag, `anyOf /vip (/score
      | gt 95)`. Each modifier is a condition computed at the call or a
      quote that answers one in its turn, run on the subject once no
      condition before it held, as for `allOf`: the first that holds
      answers `true` with the quotes after it unrun, so a flagged record
      passes without the field its quote reads, `anyOf /vip ~(/score |
      gt 95)`, and a quote that fails answers its error. The call gives
      one condition at least.
      ::link~(::any/allOf | doc) ::link~(::vec/any | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/anyOf/answer
  |~~ Whether some condition holds. ~~|
  ::boolean

:any/anyOf/conditions
  |~~ The conditions, booleans and quotes that answer them, one at least. ~~|
  ~(/)

:any/anyOf/conditions/EmptyError
  |~~ `anyOf` asks whether some condition the call gives holds, one at
      least, `anyOf /vip (/score | gt 95)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(count | gt 0)

:any/anyOf/conditions/ElementNotBooleanOrQuoteError
  |~~ Each modifier of `anyOf` is a condition computed at the call,
      `(/score | gt 95)`, or a quote that answers one in its turn,
      `~(/score | gt 95)`. A condition computed at the call is computed
      at every place, the first among them, and one that answered an
      error is refused here, the error its value: a field a record may
      lack is read softly, `anyOf (at :vip | eq true)`, and a condition
      that fails where an earlier one holds is written as a quote that
      answers it in its turn, which runs only once no condition before it
      held.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(every ~(is ::boolean ::quote))

:any/anyOf/ConditionNotBooleanError
  |~~ A quote among the conditions of `anyOf` answers a boolean when it
      runs; `:index` names, from zero, the condition whose quote answered
      another kind, and a quote that reads a field compares it to answer
      a condition, `~(/score | gt 95)`.
  ~~|

:any/anyOf/holdsWhenOneHolds
  |~~ A high score passes without the flag. ~~|
  ::law~({:vip false :score 99} | anyOf /vip (/score | gt 95))

:any/anyOf/failsWhenNoneHolds
  |~~ A low score without the flag fails. ~~|
  ::law~({:vip false :score 50} | anyOf /vip (/score | gt 95) | not)

:any/anyOf/runsAQuoteInItsTurn
  |~~ A flagged record passes without the field the quote reads. ~~|
  ::law~([{:vip true} {:vip false :score 99}]
    | map ~(anyOf /vip ~(/score | gt 95))
    | eq [true true])

:any/anyOf/answersTheFailureOfAQuote
  |~~ A quote that fails answers its error. ~~|
  ::law~({:vip false} | anyOf /vip ~(/score | gt 95) !| [type /key] | eq [::proj/KeyNotInMapError :score])

:any/anyOf/conditions/ElementNotBooleanOrQuoteError/refusesAFieldMissingAtTheFirstPlace
  |~~ A field the record lacks is refused at its place, the first among them. ~~|
  ::law~({:score 99} | anyOf (/vip | eq true)
    !| [type /index /actualType]
    |  eq [::any/anyOf/conditions/ElementNotBooleanOrQuoteError 0 ::proj/KeyNotInMapError])

:any/anyOf/conditions/ElementNotBooleanOrQuoteError/readsAMissingFieldSoftly
  |~~ A field the record may lack is read softly. ~~|
  ::law~({:score 99} | anyOf (at :vip | eq true) (/score | gt 95))

:any/anyOf/conditions/ElementNotBooleanOrQuoteError/refusesAConditionThatFailedAtTheCall
  |~~ A condition computed at the call is computed even where an earlier one holds, and its error is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~({:vip true} | anyOf /vip (/score | gt 95)
    !| [type /index /actualType]
    |  eq [::any/anyOf/conditions/ElementNotBooleanOrQuoteError 1 ::proj/KeyNotInMapError])

:any/anyOf/conditions/ElementNotBooleanOrQuoteError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number is no condition, and is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | anyOf /a
    !| [type /index /actualType]
    |  eq [::any/anyOf/conditions/ElementNotBooleanOrQuoteError 0 ::number])

:any/anyOf/ConditionNotBooleanError/refusesAQuoteThatAnswersANumber
  |~~ A quote that answers a number is refused at its place. ~~|
  ::law~({:score 99} | anyOf false ~(/score)
    !| [type /index /actualType]
    |  eq [::any/anyOf/ConditionNotBooleanError 1 ::number])

:any/anyOf/ConditionNotBooleanError/holdsAQuoteThatCompares
  |~~ A quote that compares the field it reads answers a condition. ~~|
  ::law~({:score 99} | anyOf false ~(/score | gt 95))

:any/anyOf/conditions/EmptyError/refusesNoCondition
  |~~ A call with no condition is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~({} | anyOf !| type | eq ::any/anyOf/conditions/EmptyError)

:any/unionOf
  |~~ Answers the union of the parts the call computes against the
      subject, maps or sets, in their order, a later key holding over an
      earlier: the value of a pair with its name beside it is
      `unionOf /1 {:name (/0 | keyword)}`. Maps unite with maps and sets
      with sets, a map with a set being refused by `union`, and the
      union keeps the tags of the first part. One part answers itself,
      and the call gives one at least.
      ::link~(::map/union | doc) ::link~(::set/union | doc)
  ~~|
  ~(parts | drop 1 | reduce (parts | first) ~(union))

:any/unionOf/answer
  |~~ The parts united, under the tags of the first. ~~|
  ::any

:any/unionOf/parts
  |~~ The maps or the sets, one at least. ~~|
  ~(/)

:any/unionOf/parts/EmptyError
  |~~ `unionOf` unites the parts the call gives, one at least,
      `unionOf /a /b`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(count | gt 0)

:any/unionOf/mergesTwoFields
  |~~ Two maps one value holds merge. ~~|
  ::law~({:a {:x 1} :b {:y 2}} | unionOf /a /b | eq {:x 1 :y 2})

:any/unionOf/holdsTheLaterKey
  |~~ A later key holds over an earlier. ~~|
  ::law~({:a {:x 1} :b {:x 2}} | unionOf /a /b | eq {:x 2})

:any/unionOf/foldsTheKeyIntoTheValue
  |~~ The key of a pair joins its value as a field. ~~|
  ::law~([:auth {:port 3000}] | unionOf /1 {:name (/0 | keyword)} | eq {:port 3000 :name "auth"})

:any/unionOf/unitesSets
  |~~ Two sets unite. ~~|
  ::law~({:a #[1] :b #[2]} | unionOf /a /b | eq #[1 2])

:any/unionOf/refusesAMapWithASet
  |~~ A map with a set is refused by `union`. ~~|
  ::law~({:a {:x 1} :b #[2]} | unionOf /a /b !| type | eq ::map/union/other/NotMapError)

:any/unionOf/keepsTheTagsOfTheFirst
  |~~ The union keeps the tags of the first part. ~~|
  ::law~({:a ::Box{:x 1} :b {:y 2}} | unionOf /a /b | eq ::Box{:x 1 :y 2})

:any/unionOf/answersOnePart
  |~~ One part answers itself. ~~|
  ::law~({:a {:x 1}} | unionOf /a | eq {:x 1})

:any/unionOf/parts/EmptyError/refusesNoPart
  |~~ A call with no part is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~({} | unionOf !| type | eq ::any/unionOf/parts/EmptyError)

:any/is
  |~~ Answers whether the walk of the subject's tags reaches a value of
      one of the kinds the call gives, `5 | is ::number`. A number under
      a tag of its own is a number, while `type` answers the tag over
      it; a map under a tag is a map; and a set is a vector, as a quote
      and a doc are, while a vector is no set. A failure is asked once
      `!|` catches it, an error at rest, a value of `::error` and of its
      refusal. Given
      several kinds, it answers whether the subject is of one of them,
      and given none, false. A kind no tag of the walk names is not
      reached, so a misspelt kind, `is ::Numbr`, answers false and is
      not refused. A part of the args whose invariant asks `is` is
      handed to its name as the value of the kind the walk reached, and
      one whose invariant asks `every ~(is …)` as the vector, or the set,
      of each element's, a quote and a doc as they are, an element the
      walk refuses named by its index from zero.
      ::link~(::any/type | doc) ::link~(::any/tag | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/is/answer
  |~~ `true` when the walk of the tags reaches one of the kinds, `false`
      otherwise.
  ~~|
  ::boolean

:any/is/kinds
  |~~ The kinds asked, each a tag name; of none, no value is one. ~~|
  ~(/)

:any/is/kinds/ElementNotTagError
  |~~ Each of the kinds is a tag name, `::number`; a keyword, `:number`,
      names a field and no kind.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(every ~(is ::tag))

:any/is/holdsOfItsKind
  |~~ A number is a number. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | is ::number)

:any/is/walksUnderATag
  |~~ A number under a tag of its own is a number. ~~|
  ::law~(::Width(5) | is ::number)

:any/is/walksUnderATagToAMap
  |~~ A map under a tag is a map. ~~|
  ::law~(::Box{:a 1} | is ::map)

:any/is/takesASetForAVector
  |~~ A set is a vector. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2] | is ::vec)

:any/is/takesAQuoteAndADocForVectors
  |~~ A quote is a vector of its steps, and a doc of its segments. ~~|
  ::law~([~(add 1) |~~ A page. ~~|] | map ~(is ::vec) | eq [true true])

:any/is/keepsAVectorApartFromASet
  |~~ A vector is no set. ~~|
  ::law~([1] | is ::set | not)

:any/is/asksAnOpenedFailure
  |~~ An opened error is a value of `::error` and of its refusal. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | add 1
    !| [(is ::error) (is ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError)]
    |  eq [true true])

:any/is/holdsOfOneOfSeveralKinds
  |~~ Of several kinds, the subject is one of them. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | is ::string ::number)

:any/is/failsOfOtherKinds
  |~~ A string is neither a number nor null. ~~|
  ::law~("5" | is ::number ::null | not)

:any/is/failsOfNoKind
  |~~ Of no kinds, no value is one. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | is | not)

:any/is/failsOfAMisspeltKind
  |~~ A misspelt kind answers false and is not refused. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | is ::Numbr | not)

:any/is/asksEachElementOfAPart
  |~~ The invariant of a part asks `is` of each element, a number under a tag among them. ~~|
  ::law~(:any/kindsOf ~(xs | map ~type) | :any/kindsOf/xs |~~ X ~~| ~(/)
    | :any/kindsOf/xs/ElementNotNumberError ::holds~(every ~(is ::number))
    | 5 | kindsOf ::W(1) 2 | eq [::number ::number])

:any/is/asksASetAsAVector
  |~~ A set given to a part is asked as its vector. ~~|
  ::law~(:any/setOf ~([(xs | type) (xs | map ~type)]) | :any/setOf/xs |~~ X ~~| ~(/0)
    | :any/setOf/xs/ElementNotNumberError ::holds~(every ~(is ::number))
    | 5 | setOf #[::W(1) 2] | eq [::set [::number ::number]])

:any/is/kinds/ElementNotTagError/refusesAKeyword
  |~~ A keyword is no kind, and is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | is :number
    !| [type /index /actualType]
    |  eq [::any/is/kinds/ElementNotTagError 0 ::keyword])
